<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/create_sequence_oracle.sgml
IvorySQL documentation: Oracle-compatible sequence clauses
-->

<refentry id="sql-createsequence-oracle">
 <indexterm zone="sql-createsequence-oracle">
  <primary>CREATE SEQUENCE (Oracle-compatible)</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE SEQUENCE (Oracle Compatibility)</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE SEQUENCE (Oracle Compatibility)</refname>
  <refpurpose>Oracle-compatible extensions to CREATE SEQUENCE</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE SEQUENCE name
    [ INCREMENT [ BY ] increment ]
    [ MINVALUE minvalue | NOMINVALUE ]
    [ MAXVALUE maxvalue | NOMAXVALUE ]
    [ START [ WITH ] start ]
    [ CACHE cache | NOCACHE ]
    [ [ NO ] CYCLE ]
    [ SCALE | NOSCALE ]
    [ SESSION | GLOBAL ]
    [ KEEP | NOKEEP ]
    [ ORDER | NOORDER ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>
  <para>
   In Oracle-compatible mode, CREATE SEQUENCE accepts additional clauses
   that mirror Oracle Database syntax. Some affect runtime behavior while
   others are accepted for compatibility only.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>
  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>NOCACHE</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Equivalent to CACHE 1. The sequence does not pre-allocate values.
      Each nextval call performs a disk update. Recommended for sequences
      where value gaps are unacceptable.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>NOMINVALUE</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Oracle synonym for NO MINVALUE. Sets the minimum to the data type
      lower bound (1 for ascending, min int for descending).
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>NOMAXVALUE</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Oracle synonym for NO MAXVALUE. Sets the maximum to the data type
      upper bound.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>SCALE</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Accepted for Oracle compatibility. When ivorysql.enable_seq_scale_fixed
      is enabled, appends a fixed-width instance offset to generated values
      to reduce index contention in RAC-like workloads. Without that GUC,
      this clause is parse-only.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>NOSCALE</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>Default. No instance offset is appended.</para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>SESSION</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Accepted for compatibility. In Oracle this creates a session-private
      sequence. IvorySQL parses and stores the attribute but currently
      treats the sequence as global.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>GLOBAL</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>Default. The sequence is shared across all sessions.</para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>KEEP</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Accepted for compatibility. In Oracle this retains the sequence
      value in the shared pool. IvorySQL parses the clause; caching
      behavior is controlled by the CACHE parameter.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>NOKEEP</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>Default. No special retention semantics.</para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>ORDER</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Accepted for compatibility. In Oracle RAC this guarantees ordered
      value generation across nodes. IvorySQL parses the clause; ordering
      is inherently guaranteed in a single-instance PostgreSQL backend.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term>NOORDER</term>
    <listitem>
     <para>Default. No cross-node ordering guarantee (Oracle RAC semantics).</para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>
  <para>Create a sequence with Oracle-style NOCACHE:</para>
<programlisting>
CREATE SEQUENCE order_seq START WITH 1 INCREMENT BY 1 NOCACHE NOCYCLE;
</programlisting>
  <para>Create a scalable sequence to reduce index contention:</para>
<programlisting>
SET ivorysql.enable_seq_scale_fixed = TRUE;
CREATE SEQUENCE high_traffic_seq SCALE CACHE 100;
</programlisting>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Compatibility</title>
  <para>
   These clauses are IvorySQL extensions for Oracle compatibility.
   Standard PostgreSQL does not support SCALE, SESSION, GLOBAL, KEEP,
   NOKEEP, ORDER, NOORDER, NOCACHE, NOMINVALUE, or NOMAXVALUE keywords.
  </para>
 </refsect1>
</refentry>
